  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 334 AM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nMCS and associated MCV has pushed into eastern Nebraska as of 08Z. \n850-300 mb mean winds suggests this complex will largely push east, \nbut as the complex moves east across Iowa, it is expected to take on \nmore of a northwest - southeast trajectory. Fairly substantial \noutflow extend southward from the main complex. Atmosphere appears \nto be weakly capped across northern Missouri, and have some chance \npops along the Iowa/Missouri border from 11-15Z as the main complex \nslides to the east.  South of this complex, looking at another warm \nand humid day with large upper level ridge centered across the Gulf \nCoast and broad southeasterly flow extending northward into the \ncentral US. With unseasonably mild air in place across the Central \nUS, we are once again looking at forecast highs in the 90s F and dew \npoints well in the 70s leading to heat indicies of 105-110 F across \nthe region. The only locations that may see a slight reprieve \nfrom the heat will be across northern Missouri where the outflow \nmay temporarily keep conditions cooler. \n\nArea of low pressure across western Kansas this morning is expected \nto slowly shift eastward into central Kansas by this evening.  Ahead \nof this system, low level jet is expected to strengthen feeding\ninto the remnant outflow leading to additional thunderstorm \ndevelopment across northeast KS, lifting northeast into northern\nMissouri. Precipitable water values are near the daily max \naround 2 inches, and very deep warm cloud depths of 12-13K feet \nwill support very efficient warm rain processes. Flash flood \nguidance across northern Missouri is generally in the \n2-2.5\"/3hr and 3-4\"/6 hr time scales. If storms were to train \nalong the boundary, this could be quite problematic, though \nthere are few solutions to suggest this would be a possibility. \nAt this point, it appears the bulk of the precipitation is going\nto be focused farther north where the cap is a bit weaker, but \nwill need to keep an eye on the flood potential across northern \nMissouri in the coming days as warm front associated with the \nslow moving low pressure system is expected to remain in \nvicinity of the Iowa/ Missouri border. \n\nLatest guidance suggests the low pressure system across KS is \nexpected to slowly lift northward into the Upper Midwest this \nweekend, but eastern KS and Missouri remain in the warm sector \nleading to heat continuing through at least the first half of the \nweekend.  The cold front associated with this low is expected to \nbuild southward across the region on Sunday, potentially leading to \nshowers and thunderstorms and potentially some relief from the heat. \n\nCooler conditions (potentially below normal temperatures) are \nexpected early next week, but heat and humidity return mid-to late \nweek in a return to a very similar pattern than we are currently\nin.",
